+## Remarks
+
+For a bounded two-dimensional example, take $N_0=3$ and $N_1=2$. The first
+index labels columns and the second labels rows. The grid lines below mark
+cut locations; they do not indicate that every boundary belongs to both
+neighboring cells. Precisely,
+$Q_{(r,s)}=(c_{0,r},c_{0,r+1}]\times(c_{1,s},c_{1,s+1}]$: each cell excludes its
+left and lower faces and includes its right and upper faces, subject to both
+coordinate inequalities. Thus a point on an internal vertical cut belongs to
+the column on its left, and a point on an internal horizontal cut belongs to
+the row below it, as in step 1.2.
+
+```tikz
+\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm]
+\fill[blue!35!black] (2,0) rectangle (4,1.6);
+\draw (0,0) rectangle (6,3.2);
+\draw (2,0) -- (2,3.2);
+\draw (4,0) -- (4,3.2);
+\draw (0,1.6) -- (6,1.6);
+\node at (1,0.8) {$Q_{(0,0)}$};
+\node at (3,0.8) {$Q_{(1,0)}$};
+\node at (5,0.8) {$Q_{(2,0)}$};
+\node at (1,2.4) {$Q_{(0,1)}$};
+\node at (3,2.4) {$Q_{(1,1)}$};
+\node at (5,2.4) {$Q_{(2,1)}$};
+\node[below] at (0,-0.1) {$c_{0,0}=a_0$};
+\node[below] at (2,-0.1) {$c_{0,1}$};
+\node[below] at (4,-0.1) {$c_{0,2}$};
+\node[below] at (6,-0.1) {$c_{0,3}=b_0$};
+\node[left] at (-0.1,0) {$c_{1,0}=a_1$};
+\node[left] at (-0.1,1.6) {$c_{1,1}$};
+\node[left] at (-0.1,3.2) {$c_{1,2}=b_1$};
+\node[above] at (3,3.4) {$B(a,b)$};
+\end{tikzpicture}
+```
+
+The six half-open cells are disjoint and fill $B(a,b)$, and the displayed
+volume identity specializes to
+$\mathrm{vol}(B)=\sum_{r<3}\sum_{s<2}\mathrm{vol}(Q_{(r,s)})$.
+The proof above establishes the general result independently of this picture.
